1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
URL matchers catch coarse gates:
authenticated, has role ADMIN, permit
health checks.

2
00:00:06,120 --> 00:00:19,224
They struggle with rules that need method
arguments — “release this cargo only if
the principal owns it,” “export the
manifest only if the vessel id matches a
claim.” Method security moves those
decisions onto the servic

3
00:00:19,343 --> 00:00:23,567
e layer with the same Authentication you
already established.

4
00:00:23,687 --> 00:00:33,983
Spring enables it with
@EnableMethodSecurity (modern) or older
@EnableGlobalMethodSecurity. Under the
hood, Spring AOP proxies the bean.

5
00:00:34,103 --> 00:00:42,936
Before the target method runs, an
interceptor evaluates @PreAuthorize,
@PostAuthorize, @Secured, or JSR-250
@RolesAllowed.

6
00:00:43,055 --> 00:00:55,727
SpEL expressions can reach authentication,
method parameters by name, and beans you
expose for custom checks. For harbor gate
release, ownership is the rule that path
matchers cannot express cleanly.

7
00:00:55,847 --> 00:01:05,111
Look at this java for a moment.
@PreAuthorize runs before the call — use
it to block work that should never start,
like opening someone else’s boom.

8
00:01:05,231 --> 00:01:14,352
@PostAuthorize runs after and can veto
based on the return value — useful for
read paths where you load then confirm
visibility, with care about side effects.

9
00:01:14,471 --> 00:01:29,327
@PreFilter / @PostFilter trim collections
in place; know they mutate return values
and can surprise callers. Method security
does not replace HTTP security. Filters
still authenticate and apply path rules.

10
00:01:29,447 --> 00:01:35,015
Method rules add defense in depth and
express domain authorization where the
arguments live.

11
00:01:35,135 --> 00:01:46,536
If someone invokes releaseGate from a
messaging listener or another bean inside
the JVM, the HTTP matcher never ran — the
method annotation still can, as long as
the call goes through the Spring proxy.

12
00:01:46,655 --> 00:01:59,111
Self-invocation inside the same class
bypasses the proxy, same AOP footgun you
met earlier. A misconception is annotating
controllers only and believing services
are safe when called from adapters.

13
00:01:59,231 --> 00:02:04,679
Put the rule on the service that owns the
invariant when multiple entry points
exist.

14
00:02:04,799 --> 00:02:13,487
Another is writing SpEL so complex nobody
can audit it — extract
@gateSecurity.isOwner(...) style beans so
security reviews stay readable.

15
00:02:13,607 --> 00:02:20,351
We can now deny an authenticated user on a
specific business operation, not only on a
URL prefix.

16
00:02:20,471 --> 00:02:30,527
Browser-based harbor consoles that use
cookies for the session still have a
different attack to respect: a forged
request from another site that rides the
user’s cookies to release cargo.

17
00:02:30,647 --> 00:02:36,215
That attack is CSRF — and it needs its own
defense, not another @PreAuthorize.
